# `tot fmt` writes LF — see "Canonical form" in SPEC.md. On a CRLF checkout the formatter
# would rewrite every file on sight and `tot fmt --check` could never pass, because git would
# convert the LF output straight back to CRLF. Checking out with LF is what keeps the
# formatter and the working tree agreeing, on Windows as well as anywhere else.
*.tot text eol=lf
*.tott text eol=lf
*.rs text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
# The site's sources. Not for the formatter's sake, since nothing reads these, but so that a
# Windows checkout and a Linux one hold the same bytes the way the rest of the tree does.
*.astro text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf

.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
LICENSE-MIT text eol=lf
LICENSE-APACHE text eol=lf
